use /lib

from /scope/planning import Planning
from /scope/prompt-mode/chat-input import ChatInput
from /scope/sandbox import Sandbox
from /scope/harness-integration import HarnessIntegration
from /scope/project-directory import ProjectDirectory
from /scope/project-locations import ProjectLocations
from /scope/prompt-mode/mode-system-prompts import ModeSystemPrompts
from /scope/run-targets import RunTargets

export feature ProjectData:
    pitch:
        What the application keeps for a project, kept in the project itself so
        it survives a restart and travels with the project.

    description:
        Everything the application keeps for a project lives in a `.suspense`
        directory inside the @{ProjectDirectory}, created as it is needed.
        Nothing about a project is kept anywhere else, apart from the draft.

    contents:
        - `history/`: every task sent, as its hidden anchor's source, named by the second it was sent and the anchor's name, and beside it, once its run is over, a `.json` record of the same name holding the compiled userPrompt the harness received, the system prompt as it received it and the harness it went to, whether it has been marked done by hand for sending to the other mode, each line the harness printed, each message sent to the task while it ran, in its place among those lines, any error, when the task was sent and when it ended, and beside it the harness's session for its conversation as the run left it, a `.session` folder of the same name, as the @{HarnessIntegration} says, and when each of its phases began and ended, as the debug log names them, to the millisecond, and, in a git repository, the hashes of the two snapshots of the working tree taken as it started and ended, as the task snapshots below say
        - `snapshot-index`: in a git repository, the private index the task snapshots are built with, as they say
        - `images/`: every image attached to a task or question, saved when it is sent or queued, named by the second it was saved and a short hash of its content, with its own extension, so the same image attached twice is saved once
        - `attachments/`: every file other than an image attached to a task or question, saved when it is sent or queued, under its own name, in a folder named by the second it was saved and a short hash of its content, so the same file attached twice is saved once, as the @{ChatInput} says
        - `asks/`: every question asked from the Ask tab, saved the same way as a task but apart from them
        - `queue/`: each queued prompt, as its hidden anchor's source, imports included, named so they list in the order they were queued, marked when each was queued rather than when it was saved, so prompts queued one straight after another keep their order however long each takes to save, and a chain's step marked when its chain was sent, so it lists in the place the chain's steps take in its lane's queue
        - `planning/`: the project's stories, a file per story holding its hidden anchor, and `order.json` listing the Draft, Backlog, Next sprint, Current sprint, and Done sections and their stories in order, a plan saved before Draft existed loading with Draft empty, as the @{Planning} says
        - `divergence/`: each divergence report, a JSON file named by when it was made
        - `system-prompts/`: each mode's system prompt template, a Markdown file named after its mode, code.md, combined.md for Chain, spec.md, and ask.md, and the spec-reading prompt injected into them, spec-reading.md, and the resume prompt given to a resumed task, resume.md
        - `commit-notes.json`: the Git panel's commit notes
        - `run.json`: the project's run targets, as the @{RunTargets} says
        - `settings.json`: the project's own settings, as the harness its runs go to, as the @{HarnessIntegration} says, whether its Spec runs may read the code, and whether they may read the whole project, as the @{Sandbox} says
        - `harness.json`: the last report the harness gave of the skills and agents it has
        - `fluency.md`: the project's Piton fluency, as `piton agent --print-fluency` printed it at the last spec build, generated rather than edited, for the Spec and Chain prompts to point at, as the @{ModeSystemPrompts} says
        - `suspense-fluency.md`: the Suspense fluency, as this application ships it, generated rather than edited, for the Code, Chain, Spec, and Ask prompts to point at, as the @{ModeSystemPrompts} says
        - `conversations.json`: which conversation of each lane's tasks, the spec lane's and the code lane's, and of the questions, was last left for a new one, as the @{HarnessIntegration} says, so it isn't carried on again once the project is reopened
        - `sandbox.json`: the project's classification of its paths as code or spec, its code roots, spec roots, and patterns, as the @{Sandbox} says, kept with the project

        The draft, `.suspense-draft.pi`, isn't in `.suspense`: it is the prompt
        being written, as `piton lsp` is shown it, a document named as if it
        were in the spec location, as the @{ProjectLocations} says, so the
        server resolves its imports.

    machineOnly:
        Some of it belongs to one machine rather than to the project: the
        harness report `.suspense/harness.json`, the fluency
        `.suspense/fluency.md`, printed by this machine's piton, the Suspense fluency
        `.suspense/suspense-fluency.md`, written by this machine's Suspense,
        the queue
        `.suspense/queue/`,
        the commit notes `.suspense/commit-notes.json`, the conversations left
        `.suspense/conversations.json`, whose conversations only this machine's
        harness knows, the snapshot index `.suspense/snapshot-index`,
        and the draft
        `.suspense-draft.pi` wherever it is, should it ever be written. Git
        ignores these, so they are never committed. Everything else, the prompt
        history, the questions, the divergence reports, the system prompts,
        and the sandbox classification, is kept with the project.

        The harnesses' logins belong to this machine and the user, kept where
        each harness keeps them on the host, never in `.suspense`. Each run's
        sandbox is made for it and removed after it, its temporary directory
        and filtered repository included, so nothing of it stays.

    taskSnapshots:
        In a project that is a git repository, every task the harness runs
        keeps a record of the files that changed while it ran. When the harness
        actually starts the task, not when it is sent or queued, the project's
        working tree is snapshotted, and it is snapshotted again when the run
        is over, however it ends: done, failed, or cancelled. In a project that
        isn't a git repository, nothing is snapshotted or recorded, and nothing
        about it is shown anywhere.

        A snapshot is a git tree, made with git's plumbing alone: `git add -A`
        and then `git write-tree`, run with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` pointing at a
        private index, `.suspense/snapshot-index`, so the repository's own
        index, HEAD, branches, stash, and working tree are never read from or
        written to. The private index is kept between snapshots, so each one
        hashes again only the files whose timestamps changed since the last.
        A snapshot follows `.gitignore`, as git does, so ignored files,
        `.suspense`'s machine-only data among them, are never in it. No commit,
        branch, worktree, or stash is ever made, nothing is added to the
        user's history, and no tool but git is needed.

        Both trees are pinned so git's garbage collection never deletes them,
        each under a private ref, `refs/suspense/<task>/before` and
        `refs/suspense/<task>/after`, `<task>` being the task's hidden anchor's
        name, and their hashes are kept in the task's history record. A
        snapshot that can't be taken, as when git fails, is left out rather
        than stopping the task; a task missing either snapshot shows no changed
        files.

        The private index and the refs belong to this machine: `snapshot-index`
        is ignored by git like the other machine-only data, and the refs are
        never pushed, since nothing pushes `refs/suspense/`.

    reading:
        What can't be read is left out, or shown as missing, rather than
        stopping the rest from being read.

    lastProject:
        When the application starts, it opens the project that was opened most
        recently, the first of the @{ProjectDirectory}'s recent projects
        that still holds its piton.config.pi. With none, it starts with no
        project open.

